    },
    "description": "## Introduction\nThe northern pudu (Pudella mephistophiles, Mapudungun püdü or püdu, Spanish: pudú, Spanish pronunciation: puˈðu) is a species of South American deer native to the Andes of Colombia, Venezuela, Peru and Ecuador. It is the world's smallest deer and is classified as Data Deficient in the IUCN Red List. Originally classified under genus Pudu, some authorities consider it to belong to a separate genus (Pudella) from the southern pudu, along with Pudella carlae.\n ### Physical Attributes\nThe northern pudu is the smallest species of deer in the world, standing 32–35 cm tall at the shoulder and weighing 3.3–6 kg. The antlers of the northern pudu grow to about 6 cm long and curve backward. Its coat tends to be lighter than that of the southern pudu, but the face is darker compared to the coat.",
    "wikiUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=12540881",
    "imageUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pudu%20mephistophiles.png?width=1000"
  },
