      "images": [
        "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shammy.jpg?width=1000"
      ],
      "maleImage": null,
      "femaleImage": null
    },
    "description": "## Introduction\nThe water deer (Hydropotes inermis) is a small deer species native to Korea and China. It has prominent tusks, similar to those of musk deer. It was first described to the Western world by Robert Swinhoe in 1870.\n ### Behavior\nApart from mating during the rutting season, water deer are solitary animals, and males are highly territorial. Each buck marks out his territory with urine and feces. Sometimes a small pit is dug and it is possible that in digging, the male releases scent from the interdigital glands on its feet. The male also scent-marks by holding a thin tree in his mouth behind the upper canines and rubbing his preorbital glands against it. Males may also bite off vegetation to delineate territorial boundaries.",
    "wikiUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=1765503",
    "imageUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shammy.jpg?width=1000"
  },
