    "description": "## Introduction\nThe southern pudu (Pudu puda, Mapudungun püdü or püdu, Spanish: pudú, Spanish pronunciation: puˈðu) is a species of South American deer native to the Valdivian temperate forests of south-central Chile and adjacent Argentina. It is classified as Near Threatened in the IUCN Red List.\n ### Physical Attributes\nThe southern pudu is characterized by being the second smallest deer in the world. It is slightly larger than its sister species, the northern pudu, being 35–45 cm tall at the shoulder and weighs 6.4–13.4 kg. The antlers of the southern pudu grow to be 5.3–9 cm long and tend to curve back, somewhat like a mountain goat. Its coat is a dark chestnut-brown, and tends to tuft in the front, covering the antlers. Southern pudu (Pudu puda) male head Chiloe.jpgmale Southern pudu (Pudu puda) female head Chiloe.jpgfemale",
    "wikiUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=19024891",
    "imageUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Poudou%20IMG%204372.JPG?width=1000"
  },
